($bc14530$life-big-life-calls-20$bc14530$,$bc14531$large_gap_both_discontent$bc14531$,$bc14532$Case dismissed. The accounts answer practical needs while trust stays free to grow at its own pace.$bc14532$,$bc14533$[A] = more anxious about combining finances; [B] = more comfortable.$bc14533$,$bc14534$[A] hears combining finances as potential control or exposure. [B] hears continued separation as a sign that shared goals may never become fully real. Repeating the symbolism will not solve the design. Assign money to purposes, not relationship status. Create one shared operating account, one shared goal account, and protected personal accounts. Review behavior and usefulness quarterly. The structure can become more or less connected as evidence changes.$bc14534$,$bc14535$Define the personal account protections and the actions that would count as financial control. Define the shared goals that genuinely need pooled money and the contribution rule that feels sustainable.$bc14535$,$bc14536$Case dismissed. The accounts answer practical needs while trust stays free to grow at its own pace.$bc14536$,$bc14537$Approved v4 template$bc14537$,$bc14538$Shared Discovery$bc14538$,$bc14539$[A] = more anxious about combining finances; [B] = more comfortable.$bc14539$,$bc14540$Define the personal account protections and the actions that would count as financial control.$bc14540$,$bc14541$Define the shared goals that genuinely need pooled money and the contribution rule that feels sustainable.$bc14541$,$bc14542$Both of you save a Purpose-Based Money Structure with operating account, goal account, personal accounts, contribution rules, and quarterly review.$bc14542$,$bc14543$Case dismissed. The accounts answer practical needs while trust stays free to grow at its own pace.$bc14543$);
